@prefix review: <http://example.org/orexis/review#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/review#> .
@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix orexis:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix progression: <http://example.org/orexis/progression#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/review> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis self-review" ;
    rdfs:comment """An agent re-picking a belief, inside the room it was given. A belief is not a constant: it is a POINT CHOSEN INSIDE A RANGE, because a computer needs one value to act on. So genesis picks one and the agent lives with it — and what genesis wrote is the first pick, not a bound. Treating it as a bound confuses a choice with a constraint, and leaves the author's real job, saying how much room the agent has, unwritten.""" .

#################  The capability  #################
#  The axis is WHO DOES THE JUDGING. A review is arithmetic over evidence followed by a choice,
#  and only the choice can be made differently — which is the test for whether something deserves
#  to be a capability at all. Where nothing could differ you have a function.
#
#      review:Reckoning   the AGENT works it out, from rules the packages ship
#      review:Consulting  it ASKS something else — a model, an oracle, a person
#
#  This was in the kernel until it was not. The argument that put it there was that capabilities
#  are derived from wiring and nothing in the wiring implies self-review — but capabilities are
#  worked out at genesis and wiring is one input, and there IS a premise here: an agent given room
#  to move must be able to use it. See rules.ru.

:ReviewCapability a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Review capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "The family. Rules true of reviewing however it is done target this class, so nothing is restated per implementation, and one capability may ask for 'whoever reviews' without knowing which way it does it." .

:Reckoning a :ReviewCapability ; rdfs:label "Reckoning" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent reaches the judgement itself, by running the SPARQL rule the package that owns a term ships for it. Deterministic, inspectable, and answerable: every decision traces to a rule someone wrote and a graph anyone can read." .

:Consulting a :ReviewCapability ; rdfs:label "Consulting" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent asks something else to choose — a language model, an oracle, a person. RESERVED: declared so the vocabulary is honest that the judgement is the replaceable part, but no rule grants it and no module implements it. Whatever answers would still be held to the same range and the same shapes, so it could be wrong and not unsafe. Adding it is a directory." .

:reviewIntervalS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "review interval (s)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The FLOOR between two arisings, not the schedule. A review plans its own next one from when its decision could possibly show an effect — checking sooner than a fresh window of readings can accumulate is looking at the same evidence twice. This figure only stops it planning something absurdly soon. Required of an agent that reviews, exactly as sensing:fastSleepS is required of one that subscribes; what says whether an agent reviews at all is whether it was given any room to move." .

#################  What may be re-picked, and how far  #################
#  ONE triple per revisable belief, in the ontology of the package that owns the term, naming
#  the constitutional figures that bound it. Nothing lists them and no Python knows them: the
#  reviewer finds them by asking the merged T-Box, which is what keeps adding a capability the
#  act of adding a directory. A package whose ontology nobody here has read is reviewable on
#  exactly these terms.

:RevisableBelief a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Revisable belief" ;
    rdfs:comment "A belief term an agent may re-pick while running. Marking one is a statement about the TERM, made once by the package that defines it — never about an agent, and never in a world." .

:revisableToward a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :RevisableBelief ;
    rdfs:label "revisable toward" ;
    rdfs:comment "A constitutional figure this belief may be moved toward. Stated once per end, and which end is which is read from the VALUES rather than from the property name — so a package names the two limits it already declares and invents no ordering vocabulary." .

#################  The room an agent was given  #################
#  A MANDATE, and it is the world's to state. It was briefly private, on the reasoning that how
#  far an agent will let itself move is its own opinion — which gets it backwards. A range is
#  what an agent is ALLOWED, imposed by whoever ratified its world; the pick inside the range is
#  what it wants, and that stays private. An agent constraining itself is not a constraint.
#
#  Being public is what makes the granting rule possible at all: capabilities are worked out
#  from the world, so a mandate had to be somewhere a derivation could see it.

:Mandate a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Mandate" ;
    rdfs:comment "How far one agent may re-pick one term. Stated in the world by whoever ratified it, because latitude granted is a governance fact and not an opinion. Its presence is what grants the review capability: room to move, and the ability to use it, are the same decision said once. Named for what everyone already called it: 'Commitment' collided twice — vf:Commitment is a promised economic flow, which is the CLAIM (settlement-speaks-rea), and BDI's commitment is an INTENTION — see knowledge/decisions/a-mandate-is-not-a-commitment.md." .

:commits a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :Mandate ;
    rdfs:label "commits" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reads as what the world commits this agent to — the range it may move within, and by stating it, the grant of the capability to move at all." .
:limitedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :Mandate ;
    rdfs:label "limited to" ;
    rdfs:comment """What the EQUIPMENT allows this agent, as against what the world does. The third source `ranges()` has always named and never had: constitution, mandate, hardware.

Shaped like a commitment and deliberately not one. Both narrow the same term the same way, so they intersect by the same arithmetic — but a mandate is a governance fact somebody ratified and could have written differently, and this is a fact about a board that nobody chose. Collapsing them would make `review:commits` mean two things and lose which one refused a revision.

DERIVED, never stated. `capabilities/sensing/rules.ru` computes it from the sensors an agent polls, so it lands in the derived graph like every other conclusion and a world stating it by hand is stating something it does not know.""" .

:onTerm a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Mandate ; rdfs:label "on term" .
:notBelow a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:label "not below" .
:notAbove a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:label "not above" .

#################  What an agent observed, summarised — a BELIEF about history  #################
#  The series lives in Influx. What the store keeps is fixed-size and derived: an agent does not
#  remember its readings, it remembers what they came to. That is the difference between a log
#  and a belief, and why this is in the store at all.
#
#  Here rather than in the kernel because summaries exist AS EVIDENCE FOR A REVIEW and for
#  nothing else — an agent given no room to move has nothing to weigh and keeps none. They are
#  folded in through `Module.on_reading_recorded`, the hook every module already gets, so the
#  ingest path knows nothing about them. (Whether they belong to sensing instead is issue #65
#  and a separate argument.)

:SummariesGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Summaries graph" ;
    orexis:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/summaries/" ;
    rdfs:comment "What ONE agent's readings came to. Apart from :sensed on purpose, and the separation is what makes the write boundary checkable: :sensed is the measurement record, written by whatever observed and never by a review, while a summary is the agent's own derived account of its own past. In one graph, 'a review does not touch the sensed record' would have been untestable — and an invariant nothing can check is a comment." .

:ObservationSummary a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Observation summary" ;
    rdfs:comment "Everything observed of one property of one subject over one window, as running totals rather than samples. Constant size whatever the window holds, so a belief base that summarises a year is the same size as one that summarises an hour." .

:sampleCount a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "sample count" .
:sampleMin a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ; rdfs:label "sample min" .
:sampleMax a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ; rdfs:label "sample max" ;
    rdfs:comment "Kept alongside the sums because EQUALITY WITH THE MINIMUM is the one thing a variance cannot say. A variance approaches zero for a nearly-still world and IS zero only for an instrument that has not moved at all, and those two want opposite responses." .
:sampleSum a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ; rdfs:label "sample sum" .
:sampleSumSquares a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ;
    rdfs:label "sample sum of squares" ;
    rdfs:comment "With the count and the sum, this gives the mean and the variance without keeping a single reading." .
:firstAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "first at" .
:lastAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "last at" .
:windowSeq a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ObservationSummary ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "window sequence" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which completed window this is, counting up. The accumulating one has none. Older windows past the ring are dropped, so the count of nodes is fixed and the newest is simply the largest." .

#################  What a review saw, derived at each arising  #################

:EvidenceGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Evidence graph" ;
    orexis:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/evidence/" ;
    rdfs:comment "The reviewer's own scratch: statistics derived from the summaries, plus the range each revisable term may move in. Rebuilt at every arising and never read by anything else, so a rule can be plain SPARQL over well-known terms instead of arithmetic." .

:Evidence a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Evidence" ;
    rdfs:comment "What one property of one subject has been doing. Every figure here is computable without knowing what the property MEANS, which is what lets one rule serve a domain nobody has written yet." .
:ofProperty a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Evidence ; rdfs:label "of property" .
:sampleMean a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Evidence ; rdfs:label "sample mean" .
:sampleSpread a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Evidence ; rdfs:label "sample spread" ;
    rdfs:comment "The observed range as a fraction of its own mean — unit-free, because one agent's property is a fraction and another's is degrees, and a threshold in either unit is nonsense in the other. Exactly zero means the instrument did not move." .
:sampleGapS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Evidence ; rdfs:label "sample gap (s)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Mean seconds between readings actually observed. How the reviewer works out when a fresh window could possibly exist, without knowing what it is looking at." .
:newestAgeS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Evidence ; rdfs:label "newest age (s)" .
:windowsHeld a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Evidence ; rdfs:label "windows held" .

:Residual a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Residual" ;
    rdfs:comment "One answered step of an action that predicts a number: the reading it predicted, the reading the world showed at the verdict, and where the property stood before — read off the agent's own ledger (progression:predictedValue, progression:observedValue, progression:baselineValue) and published here so a package's rule can judge its own conversion without reading the ledger. The reviewer publishes the newest ones. What a rule does with them is the package's: the market's and actuation's rules re-pick the CONVERSION when residuals lean one way and the TOLERANCE when they scatter (#518)." .
:ofAction a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Residual ; rdfs:range orexis:Action ; rdfs:label "of action" .
:predicted a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Residual ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "predicted" .
:observed a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Residual ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "observed" .
:baseline a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Residual ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "baseline" .
:verifiedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Residual ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ; rdfs:label "verified at" .
:residualWindow a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :ReviewCapability ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "residual window" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many answered steps a rule needs before it may re-pick a conversion or a tolerance from residuals — constitutional, like the reading window, because one surprising dose is noise and three are a model." .
:ReviewCapability :residualWindow 3 .

:Range a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Range" ;
    rdfs:comment "The room one term has, after the constitution's, the equipment's and the agent's mandate are intersected. Published as evidence so a rule reads a floor and a ceiling instead of re-deriving them, and so a human reading the graph can see exactly how much autonomy was in play." .

#################  What it decided, and when to look again  #################

:RevisionsGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Revisions graph" ;
    orexis:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/revisions/" ;
    rdfs:comment "What ONE agent has decided about itself. Append-only, and apart from the beliefs graph so that the beliefs graph still holds exactly one value per term — which is what every capability's shapes are written against. Deliberately NOT validated: it is the record of a decision, not a belief anyone acts on." .

:Revision a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Revision" ;
    rdfs:comment """One settled second thought, INCLUDING one settled as 'change nothing'. Recording a decline is what separates reflection from a twitch: without it an agent re-argues the same question at every arising and can never notice that it has declined eleven times and the problem is elsewhere.

**Examined against `prov:wasRevisionOf` and deliberately NOT aligned.** PROV is already spoken here — `agent/provenance.py` uses it for who put a fact in which graph — so the overlap is worth settling rather than leaving for someone to 'tidy' later.

PROV's revision is a DERIVATION BETWEEN TWO ARTEFACTS. `prov:wasRevisionOf` has `rdfs:domain prov:Entity` and `rdfs:range prov:Entity`, and `prov:Revision` is defined as *a derivation for which the resulting entity is a revised version of some original… the resulting entity contains substantial content from the original*. Its own example is `:draft_2 prov:wasRevisionOf :draft_1`.

Three things make that the wrong shape here, and the first alone settles it:

* **A decline produces no `:draft_2`.** Half of what this class exists to record is a decision to change nothing, which PROV cannot express: there is no newer entity to hang the relation from. An agent's eleven declines are the signal that the problem is elsewhere, and they would be unsayable.
* **A belief value is a literal, not an entity.** `review:fromValue 900` is a number. PROV would need a node per belief-version, and the beliefs graph deliberately holds exactly ONE value per term — see review:RevisionsGraph — because every capability's shapes are written against that.
* **Most of this class is not derivation at all.** `review:dueAt` is a schedule, `review:becauseOf` is the argument in words, `review:outcome` distinguishes declined from refused. Those describe a DELIBERATION, and PROV describes what happened to a thing.

The same finding that renamed review:Mandate away from 'Commitment' (a-mandate-is-not-a-commitment): a near-homonym, not an alignment. A reader who knows PROV would be WRONG about this data, which is the test.""" .

:revisedTerm a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Revision ; rdfs:label "revised term" .
:fromValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Revision ; rdfs:label "from value" .
:toValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Revision ; rdfs:label "to value" .
:atTime a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Revision ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "at time" .
:dueAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Revision ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "due at" ;
    rdfs:comment "When this decision is worth revisiting — far enough out that a fresh window of readings could have accumulated. It is both the memory and the schedule: the next arising is the earliest of these, and a term whose decision is not yet due is not re-argued." .
:becauseOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Revision ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "because of" ;
    rdfs:comment "The evidence, in words. An agent that cannot say why it changed its mind — or why it did not — has not reviewed anything." .
:outcome a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Revision ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "outcome" ; rdfs:comment "taken | declined | refused. Refused means the shapes rejected it, which is a bug in a rule rather than a decision an agent made." .
